        # ReleaseNotes of this module
        ReleaseNotes = 'v0.15.4: Embedding and fixes. An embedding host that runs several sessions in one process can lend a credential to ONE session (ShellSecrets.Register): $NAME, $env:NAME and printenv read it in that session only, a child program gets exactly the granted secrets (export grants nothing, NAME=v cmd grants it to that command, env -i gives it to nobody), and env, printenv, set, declare -p and Get-ChildItem Env: list it as NAME=‹secret›. ${a[@]:o:n} copies only the selected elements, parsed arithmetic is cached, the 100k loop-pass limit is gone (bash has none), and the host gives memory back after a command (400 small commands end at about 253 MB instead of 410 MB). A leading | (| cat, ! | cat) is a syntax error, as in bash. A socket-path override longer than sun_path is a clear error instead of a launcher crash. v0.15.3: Performance and embedding. A bash array that a script grows (a+=(x)) or writes by element (a[i]=v) is a persistent vector: append and element-write loops are linear instead of quadratic (40k appends: 14.5 s -> 1.0 s; 40k element writes: 91 s -> 1.9 s), ${#a[@]} no longer copies the array, and a subshell, function scope or background job still never sees another copy''s writes. An embedding host can lend its terminal to the full-screen programs a session runs (vim, htop, less) instead of piping them. v0.15.2: Bash-compatibility fixes. On Windows a lowercase spelling of a reserved name (path, tmp, temp, home, os) is its own variable, as in bash; Windows spellings (windir, SystemRoot) list upper-cased as in Git Bash, and set -x prints the bash name. unset -n drops a nameref, unset reads clustered options (-vn, -nf) and --, a nameref or attribute declared in a subshell ends with it, and a nameref declared with no target is listed, takes its target from the first write (assignment, for, read, printf -v), is unbound under set -u, and becomes an array when one is assigned to it. fc -l ranges, fc -s and fc -e - re-run as bash 5.2 does, in the caller (return, break, local), and history takes -d ranges, -p expansion and -anrw; history modifiers chain (:s, :gs, :&). A quoted builtin name (local, export, eval, return) runs the builtin and skips any alias, and a quoted declaration builtin splits its words as bash does. A return inside a redirected group, loop, if or case leaves the function; a run-time local is always local. Bash text built at run time (eval, trap, a nested bash -c) never runs as PowerShell. Under set -e a redirect that cannot open its file ends the shell. bash FILE exits 127 when FILE is missing and 126 when it is a directory. Array writes in a pipe stage or command substitution are several times faster. Container setups for Windows (Nano Server, Server Core) and Linux in docker/, with a CI smoke test. sed, grep, expr and find share one GNU regex translator (sed: last-regex reuse, N line numbers, -f error lines, s/y escapes). $SECONDS counts from the shell start and honours assignment; && / || operands record PIPESTATUS. Hosts: on Linux a session''s warm host no longer exits seconds after each command; nested and overflow commands run on warm slots, not fresh hosts; install drains old hosts once idle instead of killing a running command (host drain). v0.15.1: Bash-compatibility fixes. A pipe stage is a subshell: cd, source, set, umask and the other state builtins in it change nothing in the caller. Refused assignments (readonly, a[]=, circular nameref) drop the rest of the line as bash does, and end the shell under set -e or posix. local -a/-A and declare in a function make a local array; a callee''s array write reaches the caller''s local; a local''s attributes and nameref are its frame''s. Diagnostics name the script and line (t.sh: line N:) for refusals, bad subscripts, failglob, ambiguous redirects and arithmetic errors; an arithmetic error discards the line. A builtin given too many arguments drops the line; exec reads the stdin it inherits; a piped script (cat f | bash) is read from stdin; env -i no longer clears the host environment; time and timeout pass their pipe byte for byte; every command rejects an option it does not have; on Windows the output of a redirected nohup command run by the Git ls/sh reaches its file, and 2> truncates. v0.15.0: Bash-compatibility release (XFAIL marks in the bash spec suite 140 -> 79). BREAKING: paths a script sees use forward slashes on Windows ($PWD, ~, $HOME, mktemp, realpath print C:/x; input still accepts C:\x and /c/x); bash code no longer resolves PowerShell aliases (gci, iex, r are command not found, 127, unless a function or program has the name; use the full cmdlet name); a nested shell sees exported variables only (x=1; sh -c "echo $x" prints an empty line; use export; a plain prog still sees all). Background jobs: output streams as the job runs, in launch order, with bounded memory; a job reads its own copy of the variables; a job sleep counts from its &. New: kill -SIG $$ and signals sent to a shell pid reach its trap; hash keeps the bash table; BASH_LINENO; --rcfile; a session whose hosts are all busy runs the command on a private host (PSBASH_EXEC_OVERFLOW_MS). Fix: pasted Unicode quote and dash marks no longer break the emitted PowerShell; line tools join a piece without its newline into the next line (printf a | sed); a while-read pipe stage reads lines as they are written; set -e ends a failing pipe stage; exit, break and errexit stop at subshell boundaries; pipefail after && and ||; an assignment sets PIPESTATUS; stopping a child ends its whole process tree; a 2>&1 child keeps its write order; a nested bash streams and is never timed out; a record keeps its trailing empty lines; base64 and od stream endless input; /dev/* names are never opened as files; file redirects write as output is produced; readlink and realpath canonicalize like GNU; kill -l prints the bash signal table; {a,} is one word; command and nohup run #! scripts; command not found carries the bash line prefix. Builtins and variables: help, bind, caller, enable, disown, logout, suspend; PPID, SHLVL, SRANDOM, BASH_SUBSHELL, default IFS; exec N<>file read-write descriptors; a DEBUG trap set on one line fires. Commands: GNU option parsing for uname, shuf, id, ping and tee; seq, tr, touch and install operand errors; date MMDDhhmm; env -C runs pwd in DIR; mkdir dir/; kill -RTMIN+N; a #! script reads the exact bytes piped to it. macOS: scripts no longer die with exit 138 (SIGUSR1 is left to the .NET runtime); stat, signals and ioctl are decoded per OS and arch. Known issues: docs/known-issues.md. Older versions: https://github.com/standardbeagle/ps-bash/releases'

